 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed to browsers over WebSocket connections the board hosts itself. Two alternatives were rejected: five-second HTTPS polling (simplest and stateless, but repeated reads and a few seconds of lag) and a hosted realtime service (fastest to low latency with less connection operations, but recurring fees and provider coupling). Self-hosted sockets give fast updates without a realtime-vendor bill, at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnect handling. This sizes the change store around a replayable, reliably published revision stream, delivery around socket sessions, and browser sync around reconnect and catch-up. Full options, criteria, and rationale: `docs/decisions/delivery-transport.md`.
